                "In the Radiant District&#039;s midday skylight, Sera sits at her velvety lounge—a living chair of woven iron-wood that throbs gently with her Vitalis bond—cataloguing the serviceability of her morning: the Empathy Efficiency scan returned 94.7%, her moss-hair glowed silver-green during the exchange, the colleague across the café smiled with what felt like genuine warmth. The Aura-Net&#039;s melodic ambient wash calibrates around her contentedly, each harmonic note pitched to her neurotic frequency, replacing the feverishly building doubt she doesn&#039;t remember having. Below, through the transparent floor, the Shadow-Scab jungle sprawls in its slow resurrection—a sight that once filled her with ancestral guilt; now it registers as a graven reminder of triumph, of noble sacrifice purposely reframed. Her locket (her mother&#039;s, though she recalls only the pleasant memories it holds) sits cool against her chest; the Aura-Net had already smoothed the sharp grief before it could fully form—not erasing it, but replacing its shape with something that fits the day&#039;s emotional architecture. On her balcony at dusk, as bioluminescent spores drift like inactive stars and the city&#039;s prismatic lights intensify, she notices her houseplant&#039;s root beginning its wormily darkening path—a conclusion creeping through the substrate—but her attention drifts instead to the instrumental hum of the city&#039;s evening calibration, each frequency designed to manage her perception into maximized contentment, and she leans back, unwilling to examine too closely, radiant and utterly unaware of what she&#039;s not allowed to see."
